-- 136: GATE N2-B -- the two-seat tape is found, the predicted-vs-confirmed confounder is SETTLED BY MEASUREMENT,
-- and the blockage moves from missing data to arc-loader coverage. Follows 117/119/120/122/124/130/132/134.
-- Source doc: mvc-live-skins-quarters/docs/SUPERGUN-NETCODE.md s14.
-- Method step 4. Lane numbering: SUPERGUN NETCODE takes EVEN numbers from 120 up.
-- Apply with tools/re_kb/apply_seed.py after the README export backup (re_kb_data/_exports/*_pre136.surql).
-- Idempotent and order-independent. No apostrophes in literals. Do NOT re-run 07_dedup_edges.surql afterwards.
USE NS re DB kb;

UPSERT source:supergun_gate_n2b_20260904 SET kind='measurement', strength='reproduction', ref='tape shake.json.gz (prod key 76561198029172402_76561198289004472_76561198029172402_59618234, agent 0.3.50, stage 8, 5112 frames, rollbacks 938); harnesses d3dcap/net/sg_n2.py (--seat added) and the relocating run-builder used to place the tape battle_anchor into the stage-9 template arena; dcram rebuilt by d3dcap/receipt/dcram_build.py from the user own arc', note='A real online match between two humans with both seats active (seat0 non-idle 77 percent, seat1 70 percent). All tape-derived artefacts are game-derived and live only under %TEMP%; nothing committed.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_tape_seat_in_is_predicted SET status='confirmed', text='MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED: the tape seat_in[1] column IS the PREDICTED remote stream and confirmed_in is the ground truth. THE DISCRIMINATOR is that the LOCAL seat cannot be predicted, so it fixes the frame alignment independently. Scanning the offset between seat_in rows (sim frames) and confirmed_in triples (GGPO frames) over 5110 overlapping frames: seat 0 agreement peaks SHARPLY at offset +1 with 97.59 percent (the true sim = ggpo + 1 alignment), while seat 1 peaks two frames LATER at offset +3 with 85.56 percent. A remote stream whose best alignment is lagged relative to the local one is a lagged stream. At the alignment fixed by the local seat, seat 0 disagrees with confirmed_in on 123 frames (2.41 percent) and SEAT 1 DISAGREES ON 1354 FRAMES (26.50 percent). The disagreements are not noise: 98.8 percent of the differing seat-1 values are EXACTLY a confirmed value from 1 to 3 frames earlier (lag 1 = 31.9 percent, lag 2 = 47.3 percent, lag 3 = 19.6 percent), which is the precise signature of the GGPO repeat-last predictor holding a stale value. CONSEQUENCE: any misprediction study must use confirmed_in. Had Gate N2-B used seat_in[1] it would have measured the GGPO repeat-last predictor AGAINST ITS OWN OUTPUT -- a gate that agrees with itself by construction wherever no rollback occurred, and one that would have FLATTERED the result rather than nulling it.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_shipped_misprediction_rate_measured SET status='confirmed', text='A REAL-WORLD NUMBER FOR THE SHIPPED NETCODE, obtained free from the same analysis. On this online match the remote seat was MISPREDICTED ON 26.50 PERCENT OF FRAMES (1354 of 5110) with 938 rollbacks over 5112 frames, and the effective one-way staleness was about 2 frames (repeat-last lag distribution 1/2/3 frames = 32/47/20 percent). Cross-checking against Gate N2-A: at depth 2 the worst measured displacement was 1.1 frames of normal movement, so the typical correction on this match was small, consistent with the match being playable. ⚠ DO NOT READ THIS AS WHAT THE SUPERGUN DESIGN WOULD SEE. These figures are GGPO WITH ITS 4-FRAME INPUT DELAY APPLIED, which is exactly the mechanism that buys the remote input extra frames of travel time. A d = 0 design deliberately gives that up, so its owd and therefore its rollback depth will be LARGER than the two frames observed here. The comparison is only fair once N2-B measures both at the same owd.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_shake_anchor_relocation SET status='confirmed', text='THE SHAKE TAPE BATTLE ANCHOR CAN BE RELOCATED INTO A FOREIGN BOOT ARENA AND PASSES ALL 18 RUNNER SELF-CHECKS, BUT THE TICK FAULTS ON ARC-BUILT DC-RAM. No dump on this machine matches the tape boot: the tape wants blk 0x15D71000, ctx 0x0FC71000, dcram 0x10071000 and the nearest template is blk 0x15AC1000, ctx 0x0FFC1000, dcram 0x103C1000. Using the tape addresses with a foreign ctx fails immediately and CORRECTLY -- the runner self-checks reported ctx[0], ctx[1], ctx[2] and ctx+0x1F81B0 all pointing at the template arena. So the anchor was relocated into the template arena instead, using the blk branch which is the falsified-safe one. TWO DELTAS APPLY because blk = arena + ((rng & 0x3F) + 0xC0) MB differs per boot: blk delta -0x2B0000 over 528 intra-blk pointers, and arena delta +0x350000 over 446 pointers into dcram, ctx and the arena. The six self-pointers at blk+0x32500+8k were ASSERTED to form a valid permutation after relocation and did: [2, 0, 4, 3, 1, 5]. DC-RAM was rebuilt from the user own arc by dcram_build.py for THIS anchor roster (cids 52, 42, 42, 44, 56, 8) and stage 8. ALL 18 SELF-CHECKS THEN PASS. BUT TICK 1 FAULTS: EXCEPTION 0xc0000005 at RIP 0x140848FF4 (RVA 0x848FF4), READ 0xFFFFFFFFDE9E9475 with RAX 0xFFFFFFFFCDCDCDCD, RDX 0x0FFC1000 (= ctx), RBX 0x15ACBC58 (= blk+0xAC58). 0x140848FF4 is inside FUN_140848EE0, the NaomiLib MODEL TEXTURE-RECORD WALK (records start at model+0x18, stride rec + 0x50 + rec[0x4C], terminator rec[0] >= 0 -- the same structure dcram_build.py replays for FUN_140844DC0). RAX = 0xCDCDCDCD IS THE ASSET IMAGE MEMSET FILL, so the frame walked a model the arc build did not populate, read the fill as a record field and dereferenced it. THIS IS ARC-LOADER COVERAGE AND BELONGS TO THE ENGINE LANE (Gate 3/4), NOT TO NETCODE; everything upstream of it now works. The mid-match anchor at frame 1430 plausibly references the --carry-e / +0x145000 class that GATE4 sA2 already handles for the river anchors.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_n2b_prime_is_vacuous SET status='confirmed', text='A FALLBACK GATE THAT LOOKED PUBLISHABLE AND WAS VACUOUS, CAUGHT BY ITS OWN CONTROL. With the real anchor not ticking, the shake tape CONFIRMED two-seat inputs were fed into the working stage-9 anchor (N2-B-prime). Both fighters visibly move under those inputs -- slot 0 travels 1050 units, slot 2 travels 1123, slot 1 loses 28 HP -- so it looked like a legitimately two-sided run, and the output looked publishable: mispredicting the remote seat gave blk differing on 24 percent of samples at depth 1 rising to 72 percent at depth 30, repeat-last bit-accuracy degrading from 76 percent to 32 percent exactly as expected, and |dpx| p50/p90/max of EXACTLY 0.000 AT EVERY DEPTH. Read at face value that says mispredicting the remote seat costs nothing at any depth: a spectacular result and a false one. THE CONTROL, run because a flat-zero result is precisely what rule M4 says to distrust: identical seat-0 inputs, seat 1 = the real shake stream versus seat 1 = all zeros, 300 ticks. Result: blk differs on 237 of 300 frames but A FIGHTER DIFFERS ON 0 OF 300, worst |dpos| exactly 0.000. SEAT 1 INPUT REACHES THE INPUT WORDS INSIDE blk BUT DRIVES NO FIGHTER -- the stage-9 anchor is a single-player context in which P2 is not player-controlled. N2-B-prime is therefore VACUOUS for the visible metric, its zeros measure nothing, and it is DISCARDED. Rule M4 (check whether the gate CAN fail before trusting that it passed) was written into RE-METHOD.md this same session and caught this result within the hour; the zeros would otherwise have been the most quotable number in the netcode document.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_gate_n2b_needed SET status='open', text='GATE N2-B, RE-SCOPED 2026-09-04 (supersedes the seed-134 wording, which said it was blocked because no tape had a non-idle remote seat). TWO OF THE THREE PRECONDITIONS ARE NOW MET AND MEASURED. (1) A two-seat tape exists: shake.json.gz, agent 0.3.50, stage 8, 5112 frames, both seats about 77 and 70 percent non-idle, 938 rollbacks. (2) Which stream is ground truth is settled by measurement rather than assumption: use confirmed_in, because seat_in[1] is the PREDICTED stream (see finding:supergun_tape_seat_in_is_predicted). (3) BLOCKED: an anchor that ticks. The tape battle anchor relocates cleanly into a foreign arena and passes all 18 runner self-checks, but tick 1 faults in the NaomiLib model-record walk on arc-built DC-RAM (see finding:supergun_shake_anchor_relocation). TWO WAYS TO UNBLOCK, and the second is preferable: either ENGINE-lane arc coverage for the models this mid-match anchor walks, or a dump_live capture taken DURING a live online match on this machine, which needs no relocation at all and would also remove the untested arena-relocation branch from the critical path. Until N2-B runs, the N2-A numbers remain the only misprediction measurement and they remain a FLOOR, since N2-A mispredicts one seat with no opponent to react.';

-- edges
